1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:03,460
You ordered the salad. And then you ordered the cake.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:07,820
Not because you forgot the salad. Because you remembered it.

3
00:00:08,119 --> 00:00:11,840
That is not a joke about willpower, and it is not a failure of discipline.

4
00:00:12,380 --> 00:00:16,700
It is a licensed transaction, and your brain has been running them your entire life without

5
00:00:16,780 --> 00:00:20,620
telling you. Psychologists call it moral licensing.

6
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:25,140
By the end of this you will know why every good choice you make quietly opens the door for a bad

7
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:27,400
one, where that accounting system came from,

8
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:31,300
and why the ledger it keeps has been lying to you since the day you were born.

9
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:33,940
Start with a study that should not work.

10
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:40,400
In 2001, Benoit Monin and Dale Miller at Princeton ran an experiment that sounds backwards.

11
00:00:40,660 --> 00:00:42,980
They gave participants a hiring scenario.

12
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:47,460
In the first round, some were asked to evaluate a set of candidates where the best choice

13
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:49,900
happened to be a woman. They picked her.

14
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:52,660
Obvious call, nothing heroic about it.

15
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:55,880
But in the second round, when a different job opened up,

16
00:00:56,040 --> 00:01:00,720
the ones who had just made that perfectly reasonable choice were significantly more likely to

17
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:04,340
favor a man for a role described as better suited to women.

18
00:01:04,739 --> 00:01:08,860
A group that had not made the earlier selection did not show the same tilt.

19
00:01:09,160 --> 00:01:12,740
Read that slowly, because the mechanism is not what it looks like.

20
00:01:12,820 --> 00:01:15,000
The first group did not become more biased.

21
00:01:15,080 --> 00:01:18,520
They already held the bias. What changed was permission.

22
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:24,220
Picking the woman in round one deposited something into an internal account, a token that said

23
00:01:24,460 --> 00:01:28,080
I am fair, I am the kind of person who does the right thing.

24
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:31,040
And that token purchased exactly one thing.

25
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:34,800
A pass. The next decision no longer needed to prove fairness,

26
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:39,500
because fairness had already been demonstrated, to nobody but the decider.

27
00:01:39,940 --> 00:01:42,980
Monin and Miller called this moral credentialing.

28
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:46,300
You establish a credential, and then you spend it.

29
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:49,200
Not on someone else. On yourself.

30
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:53,820
If that were one isolated finding in a hiring lab, you could set it aside.

31
00:01:54,020 --> 00:02:00,340
It is not. In 2006, Uzma Khan and Ravi Dhar at Yale ran a cleaner version.

32
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:03,700
They gave people a choice between two products.

33
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:08,120
One group chose first between a virtuous option and an indulgent one,

34
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:12,520
say a piece of fruit versus a chocolate bar. The other group skipped that step.

35
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:16,780
Then both groups faced a second, unrelated choice between a practical item

36
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:22,760
and a luxury. The group that had just picked the fruit in round one was roughly twice as likely

37
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:27,880
to pick the luxury in round two. The ones who never made the healthy choice did not show the

38
00:02:27,960 --> 00:02:30,280
same spike. Twice as likely.

39
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:34,600
Not slightly. Doubled. And it had nothing to do with the fruit.

40
00:02:34,860 --> 00:02:37,160
Nobody was rewarding themselves for eating well.

41
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:42,320
The mechanism is stranger than that. Picking the virtuous option did not create a craving for

42
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:44,980
balance. It created a credential.

43
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:49,580
And the credential whispered, almost below hearing, that the next choice was free.

44
00:02:49,660 --> 00:02:52,720
That the account had a surplus, and a surplus can be spent.

45
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:55,920
Your brain does not track your choices the way you think it does.

46
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:59,680
You believe you are building a consistent person, one decision at a time,

47
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:05,520
like laying bricks into a wall. But the system underneath is running a ledger, not a wall.

48
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:09,180
And a ledger is a fundamentally different machine, because in a ledger,

49
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:12,740
a deposit on one side licenses a withdrawal on the other.

50
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:16,560
So you go to the gym and then eat the large fries on the way home.

51
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:19,420
You work late on Monday and scroll through Tuesday.

52
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:24,140
You donate to the charity and then ignore the person who actually needs something from you in

53
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:27,880
person. Each time, the first act is not building toward anything.

54
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:32,520
It is buying something. And what it buys is the feeling that the second act does not count.

55
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:35,980
Does any of that sound like willpower failing? It is not.

56
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:40,380
The system is working exactly as it was designed to work.

57
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:44,840
The question is where that design came from, and why it ever made sense.

58
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:49,700
Go back about two hundred thousand years to a band of roughly a hundred and fifty people who

59
00:03:49,780 --> 00:03:52,800
share a camp, share kills, share fire,

60
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:57,520
and share one permanent, public record of every act any of them commits.

61
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:01,120
In that world, your behavior was not tracked by you.

62
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:03,360
It was tracked by everyone else.

63
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:09,100
The anthropologist Christopher Boehm spent decades studying how small-scale societies manage

64
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:12,860
status and fairness, and the pattern is remarkably consistent.

65
00:04:13,120 --> 00:04:16,200
Generosity was monitored. Stinginess was punished.

66
00:04:16,640 --> 00:04:21,639
Sharing a large kill, standing sentry, carrying an injured person's load,

67
00:04:21,839 --> 00:04:25,980
each of those acts earned real, visible credit in the eyes of the group.

68
00:04:26,300 --> 00:04:29,260
And that credit was not symbolic. It was convertible.

69
00:04:29,460 --> 00:04:34,700
A hunter who had shared meat reliably for a season could sit out a hunt without losing standing.

70
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:39,380
A woman who had processed hides for others through the dry months could ask for help when she

71
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:42,120
needed it and receive it without negotiation.

72
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:47,300
The system was explicit. Your contributions were seen by thirty, fifty,

73
00:04:47,380 --> 00:04:50,600
a hundred people who remembered them, talked about them,

74
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:52,880
and adjusted what they owed you in return.

75
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:59,420
Reciprocal altruism, the term Robert Trivers gave it in 1971, was not a feeling.

76
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:05,900
It was an economy, and in that economy, a deposit genuinely did entitle you to a withdrawal.

77
00:05:06,100 --> 00:05:10,860
But the withdrawal had a ceiling, and the ceiling was set by everyone who had been watching.

78
00:05:11,180 --> 00:05:15,440
Take more than the group judged you had earned and the repayment was not financial.

79
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:22,520
It was social. Gossip first, then ridicule, then the slow freeze that ended in exile.

80
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:25,220
The cost of overwithdrawal was not debt.

81
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:27,960
It was death. Notice the critical detail.

82
00:05:28,040 --> 00:05:31,500
The ledger was external. It lived in the memories of the band,

83
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:36,600
not in the head of the contributor. Other people decided when you had earned enough to take.

84
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:39,520
Other people decided when you had taken too much.

85
00:05:39,720 --> 00:05:46,340
The balance was social, visible, and enforced by reputation, gossip, and, at the extreme,

86
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:52,820
exclusion. Now move that entire system from a camp of a hundred and fifty known faces to a city

87
00:05:52,900 --> 00:05:55,740
of strangers and a bedroom with a closed door.

88
00:05:55,920 --> 00:06:01,020
The external ledger vanished. Nobody is watching your Monday gym session and your Tuesday

89
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:06,980
scrolling and comparing the two. Nobody tallies the salad against the cake and rules on whether

90
00:06:07,060 --> 00:06:13,220
the net is positive. But the machinery that evolved to track credit and authorize spending did

91
00:06:13,300 --> 00:06:17,000
not vanish with the audience. It moved indoors.

92
00:06:17,300 --> 00:06:23,500
It became internal. And an internal ledger has one catastrophic flaw that an external one never

93
00:06:23,580 --> 00:06:25,880
had. There is no auditor.

94
00:06:26,060 --> 00:06:28,620
In the band, if you tried to take more than you had earned,

95
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:31,800
forty sets of eyes corrected you before the day ended.

96
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:36,080
The system was self-correcting because the people who kept the books were not you.

97
00:06:36,160 --> 00:06:40,720
In a modern life, the person making the deposit and the person authorizing the withdrawal and

98
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:44,440
the person spending the withdrawal are all the same person.

99
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:48,920
And that person is running the oldest possible software on a problem it was never built to

100
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,680
handle, which is judging yourself fairly.

101
00:06:52,140 --> 00:06:58,380
That is the mismatch. The feeling that a good choice earns you a bad one is not irrational.

102
00:06:58,680 --> 00:07:03,760
It is a faithful reproduction of a system that worked for a hundred thousand generations.

103
00:07:04,140 --> 00:07:06,540
It is just missing the part that made it work,

104
00:07:06,620 --> 00:07:10,060
which was the tribe standing behind your shoulder saying no,

105
00:07:10,440 --> 00:07:14,180
that is not what that credit was for. So the gym buys the fries.

106
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:18,860
The donation buys the avoidance. The productive morning buys the wasted afternoon.

107
00:07:18,940 --> 00:07:23,520
And every January, the resolution to do better starts a fresh ledger that immediately begins

108
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:26,920
spending itself down, because the architecture has not changed.

109
00:07:27,140 --> 00:07:30,040
The first good day does not build the second good day.

110
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:34,660
It purchases the right to skip it. And here is the part that cuts deepest,

111
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:38,360
the part Khan and Dhar's doubling ratio makes visible.

112
00:07:38,740 --> 00:07:44,280
You do not even notice when you spend. The licensing happens below conscious decision.

113
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:49,080
You do not sit in the drive-through and think, I earned this with my workout.

114
00:07:49,240 --> 00:07:54,460
You sit in the drive-through and feel, mysteriously, that the large is fine today.

115
00:07:54,740 --> 00:07:57,940
That you deserve it. That this one does not count.

116
00:07:58,080 --> 00:08:04,200
And it happens at every scale. The diet that fails in week three because week one went so well.

117
00:08:04,280 --> 00:08:07,820
The budget that collapses after a single month of discipline.

118
00:08:07,900 --> 00:08:13,080
The relationship where you bank three kind mornings and then spend them on one sharp evening,

119
00:08:13,160 --> 00:08:16,280
as if kindness were a currency and you had built up change.

120
00:08:16,700 --> 00:08:19,460
But it does count. Every time.

121
00:08:19,540 --> 00:08:22,680
The ledger told you it was free, and the ledger lied,

122
00:08:22,920 --> 00:08:27,400
because no one is keeping it but you. So the shift is not to try harder.

123
00:08:28,100 --> 00:08:32,959
Trying harder feeds the same machine, because a harder try is a larger deposit,

124
00:08:33,080 --> 00:08:36,040
and a larger deposit licenses a larger withdrawal.

125
00:08:36,740 --> 00:08:39,979
The shift is to see that every choice is its own event.

126
00:08:40,460 --> 00:08:43,620
Separate. Closed at the moment it is made.

127
00:08:43,919 --> 00:08:47,920
Not connected to the choice before it and not funding the choice after it.

128
00:08:48,340 --> 00:08:53,020
The salad was a thing you did at noon. It has no opinion about what you do at nine tonight.

129
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:57,640
The gym was a thing you did on Monday. It does not vote on Tuesday.

130
00:08:57,780 --> 00:09:02,720
The donation was a real act of generosity, and it bought exactly one thing,

131
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:05,780
whatever the donation bought. Not a pass.

132
00:09:06,100 --> 00:09:08,760
Not a surplus. Not permission.

133
00:09:09,140 --> 00:09:13,940
One concrete move. The next time you catch yourself feeling that something is fine today,

134
00:09:14,240 --> 00:09:19,480
that you have earned it, that this one does not count, stop and ask one question.

135
00:09:20,080 --> 00:09:23,360
Would I still choose this if the good thing had not happened first?

136
00:09:23,840 --> 00:09:28,340
If the answer is no, then what you are holding is not a decision.

137
00:09:28,480 --> 00:09:33,200
It is a receipt. And the purchase it represents was made by a system that lost its auditor

138
00:09:33,280 --> 00:09:37,620
roughly ten thousand years ago and has been spending unsupervised ever since.

139
00:09:37,780 --> 00:09:41,160
You ordered the salad. And then you ordered the cake.

140
00:09:41,920 --> 00:09:45,820
Not because you are weak. Not because you lack discipline.

141
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:50,300
Because a machine that once made perfect sense in a camp where everyone was watching is now

142
00:09:50,380 --> 00:09:52,700
running in a room where no one is.

143
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:56,340
And the only person it needs to convince is you.

144
00:09:56,960 --> 00:09:59,860
And here is the question this video deliberately left open.

145
00:09:59,980 --> 00:10:05,620
The one person whose yes you imagined buying with all that good behavior, the colleague,

146
00:10:05,700 --> 00:10:10,280
the friend, the stranger, they were never checking your ledger.

147
00:10:10,360 --> 00:10:12,720
They were never even keeping one.

148
00:10:13,160 --> 00:10:19,220
So who were you performing for? There is a video on this channel called Why People Say Yes Far

149
00:10:19,300 --> 00:10:22,640
More Often Than You Think. It answers that question,

150
00:10:22,720 --> 00:10:25,460
and the answer has nothing to do with earning anything.

151
00:10:25,740 --> 00:10:27,100
Watch that one next.
